    {
      "id": "s20",
      "type": "BATCH_SWEEP",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Async genuinely wins in one particular situation. Say you need fifty pages scraped at the same time. One at a time, that is a very long wait indeed. Overlapping, the whole thing collapses into a short one.",
      "data": {
        "batchSweep": {
          "rows": 7,
          "atWord": 8.917,
          "slow": {
            "label": "One at a time",
            "stat": "50 waits",
            "color": "red"
          },
          "fast": {
            "label": "Overlapping",
            "stat": "1 wait",
            "color": "green"
          },
          "source": "illustrative"
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 411,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-03-sync-async_long_s20.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s21",
      "type": "KINETIC_TEXT",
      "transition": "glitch",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Because the thing that overlaps is the waiting, not the work. While one page is still loading, the other forty nine are already loading right alongside it.",
      "data": {
        "kinetic": {
          "text": "The waiting is what overlaps.",
          "fx": "highlight",
          "sub": "not the work — the waiting",
          "atWord": 8.75
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 272,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-03-sync-async_long_s21.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s22",
      "type": "PIPELINE_GANTT",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Drawn out across time it looks like this. Each page starts before the one in front of it has finished, so instead of adding up one after another, all of that waiting stacks on top of itself, and the total run time collapses right down.",
      "data": {
